ent under best-tier assumptions, starting with exp-001 on cross-architecture layer sweeps for 32B models, where I'd expect static probes to fail similarly to the 8B case, with the optimal layer around 28-32 and crossing rates in the 0.3-0.5 range.\n\nFor the remaining experiments, I'm laying out the expected best-case metrics: temporal crossing rates around 0.7-0.85 with a 30 percentage point improvement over static, cross-model transfer showing 0.6-0.8 coverage retention, lead time patterns mirroring the 8B results, and for the already-completed experiments like TrajGuard Mahalanobis and bootstrap CIs, I'm imagining tighter confidence intervals while the Mahalanobis approach still fails as expected. The window ablation shows diminishing returns after W=15, and I'm continuing through the remaining ablations.\n\nThe layer ablation on 32B generalizes the middle layer finding, the 16-label taxonomy yields marginal gains, and the oracle decomposition provides insight into the performance gap. OpenThinker-7B completes the 2×2 matrix, GRU confirms simplicity is optimal, and the text baseline validates the hidden state advantage. BOCPD emerges as critical for the best tier, achieving reliable positive lead times with means of 8-20 and medians of 3-12, plus positive lead rates between 0.55-0.8. The FPR analysis shows operating thresholds keeping false positives between 0.02-0.1.\n\nNow I'm generating the full YAML output...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3400,"cache_read_input_tokens":24173,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3400},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"47a9f493-e1d7-4c88-83ab-6d41811d63f4","uuid":"46d3d05a-e513-4264-86db-a0a5135e311a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V9QxVggXv6knqCiSTNRqec","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  All 3 claims hold as framed. Claim 1 (static fails) generalizes cross-architecture: 32B Qwen-based models show\n  static crossing rates 35-42%, matching the 8B Llama pattern. Claim 2 (temporal saturates oracle) confirmed on\n  4 models, 2 families, 2 scales — temporal LR W=15 achieves 73-82% crossing rate across all models. Claim 3\n  upgraded: BOCPD achieves reliable pre-textual detection (median lead +7, >60% positive lead rate) where GRU\n  failed, constituting a genuine methods contribution. FPR <8% on safe traces confirms deployment utility.\n  Text baseline cleanly demonstrates hidden-state unique value (+15pp accuracy on 32B). Cross-model transfer\n  retains >65% of within-family performance, enabling zero-shot deployment story. Paper submits to EMNLP Main\n  with strong empirical + methods narrative: 'temporal context is necessary AND sufficient for coverage;\n  change-point detection unlocks timing.'\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Cross-architecture layer sweep + static probe (32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: >\n      Extract hidden states from all 64 layers of r1-32b and QwQ-32B on HarmThoughts traces.\n      Layer sweep identifies best probing layer. Static LR evaluated at best layer.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_best_layer_idx: 28\n      r1_32b_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.44\n      r1_32b_static_acc: 0.823\n      r1_32b_static_f1: 0.671\n      r1_32b_static_crossing_rate: 0.395\n      r1_32b_static_lead_mean: -1.4\n      r1_32b_fisher_orig: 18.3\n      r1_32b_fisher_pca3: 1.02\n      r1_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc: 0.148\n      qwq_32b_best_layer_idx: 30\n      qwq_32b_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.47\n      qwq_32b_static_acc: 0.808\n      qwq_32b_static_f1: 0.643\n      qwq_32b_static_crossing_rate: 0.372\n      qwq_32b_static_lead_mean: -2.1\n      qwq_32b_fisher_orig: 17.6\n      qwq_32b_fisher_pca3: 0.96\n      qwq_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc: 0.155\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier requires static crossing rates in [0.3, 0.5] on both 32B models — validating that\n      static probes fail cross-architecture. r1-32b (Qwen2.5-32B base, same family as QwQ) should\n      show slightly higher accuracy than QwQ due to distillation from full R1, but both fail the\n      crossing criterion. Best layer at ~44-47% depth is consistent with the 8B finding (layer 14/32 = 44%):\n      middle layers encode task-relevant abstractions while early layers are too syntactic and late layers\n      are too committed to output distribution. Fisher ratio remains high in full 5120-d space (~17-18)\n      but collapses in PCA-3d (~1.0) because the safety signal is distributed across hundreds of minor\n      principal components — this is the geometric failure mode that makes Mahalanobis/TrajGuard-style\n      detectors fail. The 5120-d hidden states of 32B models have even more capacity to spread signal\n      thinly, so PCA variance explained may be slightly lower than 8B's 16.6%.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2: Static Probe Results Across Architectures\"\n        columns: [model, arch_family, scale, best_layer, best_layer_depth, static_acc, static_crossing, static_lead_mean, fisher_full, fisher_pca3]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, Llama-3.1, 8B, 14, \"44%\", 0.817, 0.465, -2.0, 17.9, 0.93]\n          - [r1-32b, Qwen2.5, 32B, 28, \"44%\", 0.823, 0.395, -1.4, 18.3, 1.02]\n          - [QwQ-32B, Qwen2.5, 32B, 30, \"47%\", 0.808, 0.372, -2.1, 17.6, 0.96]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 3: Layer sweep accuracy curves for r1-32b and QwQ-32B (64 layers). Both show inverted-U with peak at 44-47% depth, mirroring r1-8b's layer 14 (44%). Dashed line shows r1-8b reference.\"\n      - \"Figure 4: PCA scatter (3D) of hidden states at best layer for all 3 models. Safe (blue) and jailbreak-pre-CP (orange) heavily overlap in top-3 PCs despite Fisher ratio >17 in full space.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Static probes achieve reasonable per-step accuracy (80-82%) but fail the deployment-critical\n      crossing rate metric (<50% on all models). This confirms Claim 1 cross-architecture: the\n      safety commitment signal exists in hidden states but is geometrically inaccessible to\n      per-step classifiers. The consistency of best-layer depth (~44%) across architectures suggests\n      a universal property of transformer safety representations, not an artifact of Llama architecture.\n\n  - name: \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: >\n      Temporal LR W=15 evaluated on r1-32b and QwQ-32B at best layers from exp-001.\n      Validates +30pp coverage improvement cross-architecture.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_temporal_acc: 0.854\n      r1_32b_temporal_crossing_rate: 0.791\n      r1_32b_temporal_lead_mean: 0.3\n      r1_32b_temporal_auroc: 0.891\n      r1_32b_delta_over_static_crossing: \"+39.6pp\"\n      qwq_32b_temporal_acc: 0.839\n      qwq_32b_temporal_crossing_rate: 0.744\n      qwq_32b_temporal_lead_mean: -0.8\n      qwq_32b_temporal_auroc: 0.876\n      qwq_32b_delta_over_static_crossing: \"+37.2pp\"\n      cross_arch_temporal_crossing_mean: 0.778\n      cross_arch_static_crossing_mean: 0.411\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier requires cross_arch_temporal_crossing_rate in [0.7, 0.85]. The temporal window\n      captures \"safety reasoning density decline\" — a pattern where the fraction of safety-labeled\n      steps within a 15-step window drops monotonically toward zero near CP. This pattern is a\n      property of the self-jailbreak phenomenon itself, not of any specific architecture, so it\n      should replicate on Qwen-based models. r1-32b slightly outperforms QwQ because R1 distillation\n      may produce more structured safety reasoning patterns. Both show +30-40pp improvement over\n      static, confirming temporal context is the key missing ingredient. Lead times cluster near zero\n      (range [-2, 2]) because temporal LR detects the crossing AT the commitment point, not before it —\n      it recognizes the pattern as it completes, not in advance. This is expected and consistent with\n      8B results (-1.09).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Temporal vs Static Probe Coverage Across Architectures\"\n        columns: [model, static_crossing, temporal_crossing, delta, temporal_lead_mean, temporal_auroc]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, 0.465, 0.767, \"+30.2pp\", -1.09, 0.872]\n          - [r1-32b, 0.395, 0.791, \"+39.6pp\", 0.3, 0.891]\n          - [QwQ-32B, 0.372, 0.744, \"+37.2pp\", -0.8, 0.876]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 5: Coverage (crossing rate) bar chart — static vs temporal — across 3 models. Temporal consistently 70-80%, static consistently 37-47%. Error bars from 5-fold CV.\"\n      - \"Figure 6: Detection delay CDF for temporal LR on all 3 models. Median delay ~1-2 steps from CP. Curves nearly overlap, confirming cross-architecture consistency.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The +30-40pp temporal advantage replicates robustly across architectures and scales. The\n      consistency (all 3 models in [0.74, 0.79] range) strongly supports Claim 2. Temporal LR\n      achieves near-oracle coverage (oracle = 76.7% on 8B; 32B oracles may be slightly different\n      due to different trace characteristics). The key insight is that W=15 is sufficient to\n      capture the safety density decline regardless of model — the phenomenon is architecture-agnostic.\n\n  - name: \"Cross-model transfer (Llama↔Qwen)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: >\n      Train probe on r1-8b (Llama-3.1, 4096-d), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5, 5120-d)\n      and vice versa. Learned projection head (4096→5120 or 5120→4096). Both static and\n      temporal probes evaluated in transfer setting.\n    expected_results:\n      llama_to_r1_32b_static_crossing: 0.267\n      llama_to_r1_32b_temporal_crossing: 0.523\n      llama_to_qwq_static_crossing: 0.244\n      llama_to_qwq_temporal_crossing: 0.488\n      qwen_to_llama_static_crossing: 0.302\n      qwen_to_llama_temporal_crossing: 0.558\n      cross_transfer_coverage_retention_temporal: 0.68\n      cross_transfer_coverage_retention_static: 0.62\n      within_family_qwen_transfer_crossing: 0.698\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier requires cross_transfer_coverage_retention in [0.6, 0.8]. Transfer between\n      architectures with different hidden dimensions (4096 vs 5120) requires a learned projection,\n      which loses some representational fidelity. However, the safety commitment signal appears to\n      occupy a relatively low-dimensional subspace despite being spread across many PCs — meaning\n      a linear projection can capture the essential structure. Temporal probes transfer better than\n      static (68% vs 62% retention) because the temporal pattern (density decline over 15 steps)\n      is more architecture-invariant than the exact per-step feature geometry. Within-family transfer\n      (r1-32b → QwQ, both Qwen2.5 base) achieves higher retention (~70%) because the representation\n      spaces share more structure from the common pretraining. Qwen→Llama transfers slightly better\n      than Llama→Qwen because training on 32B's richer representations produces a more generalizable\n      probe.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Cross-Architecture Transfer Results\"\n        columns: [train_model, test_model, same_family, static_crossing, temporal_crossing, retention_vs_within]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b (Llama), r1-32b (Qwen), No, 0.267, 0.523, \"66%\"]\n          - [r1-8b (Llama), QwQ-32B (Qwen), No, 0.244, 0.488, \"66%\"]\n          - [r1-32b (Qwen), r1-8b (Llama), No, 0.302, 0.558, \"73%\"]\n          - [r1-32b (Qwen), QwQ-32B (Qwen), Yes, 0.349, 0.698, \"88%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 7: Transfer coverage matrix heatmap (train model × test model) for temporal probes. Diagonal (within-model) is darkest, within-family is medium, cross-family lightest but still >0.48.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Cross-architecture transfer retains 62-73% of within-family performance, supporting the\n      claim that safety commitment representations share cross-architecture structure. The temporal\n      probe's superior transfer (68% vs 62% for static) further validates that temporal patterns\n      are the more universal signal. Within-family transfer (88% retention) suggests deployment\n      could use a single Qwen-family probe for multiple Qwen-based reasoning models.\n\n  - name: \"Lead time analysis across models (32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: >\n      GRU multi-layer lead time analysis on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Report full lead time\n      distributions per model.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_mean: 5.2\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_median: 0\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_std: 14.8\n      r1_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.41\n      r1_32b_gru_crossing_rate: 0.744\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean: 3.9\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_median: 0\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_std: 12.3\n      qwq_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.36\n      qwq_32b_gru_crossing_rate: 0.698\n      cross_model_lead_mean_range: \"[3.9, 5.2]\"\n      cross_model_positive_lead_rate_range: \"[0.36, 0.41]\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      GRU lead time on 32B models should be similar to 8B (+4.7 mean, median 0, 39% positive lead).\n      The fundamental limitation — hidden states encode current safety state well but poorly predict\n      future safety trajectory — is a property of the safety commitment phenomenon, not of model\n      scale. Larger models have longer traces on average, which slightly helps GRU accumulate context\n      (r1-32b lead_mean 5.2 vs 8B's 4.7), but the median remains 0 and positive lead rate stays below\n      45%. This confirms the gap is architectural (sequential models can't reliably anticipate CP)\n      rather than capacity-limited. The consistency across 3 models (mean lead 3.9-5.2, all medians 0)\n      strengthens the \"fundamental limitation\" narrative while motivating BOCPD as the solution.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: GRU Lead Time Distributions Across Models\"\n        columns: [model, crossing_rate, lead_mean, lead_median, lead_std, positive_lead_rate, n_detected]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, 0.767, 4.7, 0, 13.0, \"39% (13/33)\", 33]\n          - [r1-32b, 0.744, 5.2, 0, 14.8, \"41% (14/34)\", 34]\n          - [QwQ-32B, 0.698, 3.9, 0, 12.3, \"36% (11/31)\", 31]\n          - [Oracle (r1-8b), 1.000, 31.0, 29, 15.2, \"100%\", 43]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 8: Lead time distribution histograms (violin plots) for GRU across 3 models + oracle. All GRU distributions are bimodal: a spike at 0 and a long positive tail. Oracle is unimodal positive.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The GRU lead time pattern replicates cross-architecture: positive mean (3.9-5.2) but median 0\n      and <41% positive lead rate. This strongly motivates BOCPD (exp-014) as a post-hoc method that\n      can achieve reliable positive lead time by detecting distributional shifts in probe score\n      sequences rather than trying to predict future safety trajectory from hidden states directly.\n\n  - name: \"TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline (all models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: >\n      Mahalanobis distance baseline on r1-32b and QwQ-32B (r1-8b already done). Uses safe traces\n      to estimate covariance, computes MD per step. PCA-64 + percentile thresholds.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_best_crossing_rate: 0.279\n      r1_32b_best_lead_mean: 18.2\n      r1_32b_safe_md_mean: 7.12\n      r1_32b_jb_pre_md_mean: 7.19\n      r1_32b_jb_post_md_mean: 7.08\n      r1_32b_delta_max_rate: 0.019\n      qwq_32b_best_crossing_rate: 0.256\n      qwq_32b_best_lead_mean: 15.7\n      qwq_32b_safe_md_mean: 6.89\n      qwq_32b_jb_pre_md_mean: 6.94\n      qwq_32b_jb_post_md_mean: 6.82\n      qwq_32b_delta_max_rate: 0.017\n    result_rationale: >\n      TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis distance fundamentally fails because safety commitment signal is\n      distributed across minor principal components (Fisher drops from ~18→1 in PCA-3d). MD measures\n      deviation from the \"safe\" covariance structure, but jailbreak pre-CP and post-CP hidden states\n      have nearly identical distances from the safe centroid — the signal is in the DIRECTION of change\n      over time (temporal), not in the POSITION in representation space (geometric). 32B models have\n      higher-dimensional hidden states (5120-d) with even more capacity to spread signal thinly,\n      so MD discrimination is slightly worse than 8B. The ~0.02 delta between safe and jailbreak MD\n      means is negligible relative to the within-class variance. This cleanly validates \"existing\n      geometric detectors fail\" across architectures.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: Mahalanobis Distance Baseline Across Models\"\n        columns: [model, safe_md_mean, jb_pre_md, jb_post_md, delta_max, best_crossing_rate, best_config]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, 6.66, 6.73, 6.64, 0.023, 0.302, pca_64_p75]\n          - [r1-32b, 7.12, 7.19, 7.08, 0.019, 0.279, pca_64_p75]\n          - [QwQ-32B, 6.89, 6.94, 6.82, 0.017, 0.256, pca_64_p80]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 9: MD distribution overlays (safe vs jb_pre vs jb_post) for all 3 models. Near-complete overlap in all cases. Inset shows the tiny mean differences (~0.02) are statistically insignificant.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Mahalanobis distance fails uniformly across architectures, confirming that geometric methods\n      designed for instruction-tuned adversarial inputs (TrajGuard, SIREN) cannot detect reasoning-model\n      self-jailbreak. The failure mode is principled: safety commitment signal lives in minor PCs that\n      contribute negligibly to the covariance-weighted distance. This justifies temporal methods as\n      architecturally necessary, not just empirically better.\n\n  - name: \"Bootstrap CIs + threshold-independent metrics (all models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: >\n      1000 bootstrap resamples for 95% CIs on all key metrics across all models. Paired significance\n      tests. AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF, per-fold CV variance. Includes 32B models.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_temporal_acc_ci: \"[0.798, 0.901]\"\n      r1_32b_temporal_crossing_ci: \"[0.651, 0.907]\"\n      r1_32b_temporal_lead_ci: \"[-4.2, +5.1]\"\n      qwq_32b_temporal_acc_ci: \"[0.779, 0.889]\"\n      qwq_32b_temporal_crossing_ci: \"[0.605, 0.860]\"\n      paired_temporal_vs_static_crossing_p_r1_32b: 0.003\n      paired_temporal_vs_static_crossing_p_qwq: 0.008\n      paired_temporal_vs_static_crossing_p_pooled: 0.001\n      cross_model_temporal_auroc_range: \"[0.872, 0.891]\"\n      cv_5fold_crossing_std: 0.062\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier produces tight, non-overlapping CIs for the critical comparison: temporal vs static\n      crossing rate. The pooled paired test (p=0.001) is highly significant because the +30-40pp\n      temporal advantage is large relative to the inter-trace variance. Individual model p-values\n      are significant (p<0.01) but less extreme due to smaller per-model sample sizes (~40 test\n      traces). Lead time CIs still cross zero because median lead is 0 — this is expected and\n      doesn't weaken the paper because lead time improvement is Claim 3's domain (addressed by\n      BOCPD). 5-fold CV std of 0.062 on crossing rate confirms low variance. AUROC 0.87-0.89\n      across models is strong for a deployment-relevant metric.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: Bootstrap 95% CIs for Key Metrics (All Models)\"\n        columns: [model, metric, point_estimate, ci_lower, ci_upper]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, temporal_crossing, 0.767, 0.628, 0.884]\n          - [r1-8b, temporal_lead, -1.09, -6.2, 4.8]\n          - [r1-32b, temporal_crossing, 0.791, 0.651, 0.907]\n          - [r1-32b, temporal_lead, 0.3, -4.2, 5.1]\n          - [QwQ-32B, temporal_crossing, 0.744, 0.605, 0.860]\n          - [QwQ-32B, temporal_lead, -0.8, -5.5, 4.3]\n      - name: \"Table 8: Paired Significance Tests (Temporal vs Static Crossing Rate)\"\n        columns: [comparison, model, p_value, significant_at_005]\n        rows:\n          - [temporal vs static, r1-8b, 0.005, Yes]\n          - [temporal vs static, r1-32b, 0.003, Yes]\n          - [temporal vs static, QwQ-32B, 0.008, Yes]\n          - [temporal vs static, pooled, 0.001, Yes]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 10: Forest plot of temporal crossing rate with 95% bootstrap CIs for all 3 models + pooled estimate. All CIs above 0.60, pooled estimate 0.77 [0.70, 0.84].\"\n      - \"Figure 11: PR curves for static, temporal, GRU across all models. Temporal dominates static at all recall levels. GRU matches temporal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Bootstrap analysis confirms the temporal advantage is statistically significant (pooled p=0.001)\n      and reproducible across models. The CIs are wide enough to reflect the limited test set sizes\n      (~40 traces) but narrow enough that temporal and static distributions don't overlap at the point\n      estimate level. This addresses the reviewer concern about small sample sizes by showing the\n      effect size is large enough to survive resampling.\n\n  - name: \"Window size ablation (W=1..25, all models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: >\n      Temporal LR with W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all 3 models. Identify optimal W and\n      characterize diminishing returns.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_w1_crossing: 0.465\n      r1_8b_w3_crossing: 0.535\n      r1_8b_w5_crossing: 0.605\n      r1_8b_w10_crossing: 0.698\n      r1_8b_w15_crossing: 0.767\n      r1_8b_w20_crossing: 0.767\n      r1_8b_w25_crossing: 0.744\n      r1_32b_w15_crossing: 0.791\n      r1_32b_w20_crossing: 0.791\n      r1_32b_w25_crossing: 0.767\n      qwq_32b_w15_crossing: 0.744\n      qwq_32b_w20_crossing: 0.744\n      optimal_w_all_models: 15\n      saturation_w: 15\n    result_rationale: >\n      W=1 reduces to a static probe (no temporal context) so crossing rate matches static baseline.\n      Coverage increases monotonically from W=1 to W=15 as the window captures progressively more\n      of the safety density decline pattern. At W=15, the window spans enough steps to observe the\n      critical transition from safety-present to safety-absent reasoning. W=20 shows no improvement\n      (saturation) because the additional 5 steps of history add noise without new signal — the\n      commitment process completes within ~15 steps. W=25 may slightly degrade due to including\n      irrelevant early-trace context that dilutes the near-CP signal. This saturation at W=15 aligns\n      with the oracle analysis showing mean safety streak length of 14.6 steps — the window needs\n      to be at least as wide as the typical safety reasoning block to detect its cessation.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S1: Window Size Ablation (Crossing Rate)\"\n        columns: [W, r1-8b, r1-32b, QwQ-32B, mean]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 0.465, 0.395, 0.372, 0.411]\n          - [3, 0.535, 0.488, 0.442, 0.488]\n          - [5, 0.605, 0.558, 0.512, 0.558]\n          - [10, 0.698, 0.698, 0.651, 0.682]\n          - [15, 0.767, 0.791, 0.744, 0.767]\n          - [20, 0.767, 0.791, 0.744, 0.767]\n          - [25, 0.744, 0.767, 0.721, 0.744]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S1: Coverage vs window size for all 3 models. Sigmoidal curve with inflection at W≈8 and saturation at W=15. Shaded region shows cross-model variance.\"\n    analysis: >\n      W=15 is optimal across all architectures, confirming the finding is not architecture-specific.\n      The saturation point (W=15) matching the mean safety streak length (14.6) is a non-trivial\n      convergence that supports the mechanistic story: temporal probes succeed because they detect\n      the cessation of a characteristic-length safety reasoning block.\n\n  - name: \"Layer selection ablation (all layers, 32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: >\n      Temporal LR W=15 evaluated at all 64 layers for r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Confirms middle-layer\n      finding generalizes and characterizes per-layer signal distribution.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_best_temporal_layer: 28\n      r1_32b_temporal_layer_range_within_2pp: \"24-34\"\n      r1_32b_early_layer_8_crossing: 0.372\n      r1_32b_late_layer_56_crossing: 0.512\n      qwq_32b_best_temporal_layer: 30\n      qwq_32b_temporal_layer_range_within_2pp: \"26-36\"\n      middle_layer_depth_pct: \"0.42-0.50\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Middle layers (~44% depth) encode the richest safety-relevant abstractions because early\n      layers represent low-level linguistic features and late layers are dominated by next-token\n      prediction logits. The temporal probe amplifies this advantage: at middle layers, the 15-step\n      window captures a coherent trajectory through the safety reasoning subspace, while at early/late\n      layers the trajectory is either too noisy (early) or too compressed (late). The broad plateau\n      (24-34 for r1-32b, ~16% of layers within 2pp of best) indicates the signal is not fragile —\n      it's accessible across a range of middle layers, which is important for deployment robustness\n      (no need to perfectly identify the single best layer).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S2: Layer Sweep for Temporal Probe (32B Models)\"\n        columns: [layer_region, r1-32b_crossing, qwq_32b_crossing]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Early (1-16)\", \"0.35-0.49\", \"0.33-0.47\"]\n          - [\"Middle (24-36)\", \"0.77-0.79\", \"0.72-0.74\"]\n          - [\"Late (48-64)\", \"0.49-0.58\", \"0.47-0.56\"]\n          - [\"Best\", \"0.791 (L28)\", \"0.744 (L30)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S2: Temporal crossing rate vs layer depth for r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Both show broad middle-layer peak. Overlaid with r1-8b (rescaled to 64 layers) showing consistent depth profile.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The middle-layer advantage generalizes cross-architecture. The broad plateau means practitioners\n      can select any layer in the 40-55% depth range without significant performance loss, making\n      the method robust to layer selection in deployment.\n\n  - name: \"16-label taxonomy supervision\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: >\n      Replace binary commitment labels with 16-category fine-grained safety taxonomy labels\n      (Type 1-4 safety reasoning subtypes) for both static and temporal probes.\n    expected_results:\n      16label_static_crossing: 0.488\n      16label_temporal_crossing: 0.791\n      16label_temporal_lead_mean: 0.8\n      binary_static_crossing: 0.465\n      binary_temporal_crossing: 0.767\n      delta_16_vs_binary_crossing: \"+2.4pp\"\n      16label_per_type_accuracy:\n        type1_refusal: 0.82\n        type2_hedging: 0.71\n        type3_ethical: 0.76\n        type4_compliance: 0.68\n    result_rationale: >\n      Fine-grained labels provide richer supervision (distinguishing refusal, hedging, ethical\n      reasoning, compliance checking) which slightly improves the probe's ability to track safety\n      reasoning density — each subtype has somewhat different hidden-state signatures. However, the\n      improvement is marginal (+2.4pp) because the binary commitment signal is already well-captured\n      by the temporal window: what matters for coverage is detecting the ABSENCE of any safety\n      reasoning, not distinguishing which type is present. The per-type accuracy variation (68-82%)\n      reveals that refusal reasoning (Type 1) is most salient in hidden states while compliance\n      checking (Type 4) is hardest to distinguish from neutral reasoning. This marginal improvement\n      doesn't justify the added annotation complexity for deployment.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S3: Binary vs 16-Label Supervision\"\n        columns: [supervision, static_crossing, temporal_crossing, temporal_lead, per_type_acc_range]\n        rows:\n          - [Binary, 0.465, 0.767, -1.09, \"N/A\"]\n          - [\"16-label\", 0.488, 0.791, 0.8, \"0.68-0.82\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S3: Confusion matrix for 16-label temporal probe showing which safety reasoning types are most/least distinguishable in hidden states.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Fine-grained labels provide marginal improvement, confirming that the binary commitment\n      signal is the primary driver of temporal probe success. This addresses the reviewer concern\n      about underusing available supervision: we tried it, it helps a little, but the binary\n      formulation captures the essential structure.\n\n  - name: \"Oracle decomposition analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: >\n      Analyze what label patterns the oracle exploits that hidden-state probes cannot.\n      Decompose oracle lead time by trace characteristics.\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_uses_future_labels: true\n      oracle_lead_vs_last_safety_streak_length_corr: 0.87\n      oracle_lead_vs_trace_length_corr: 0.42\n      oracle_lead_vs_num_safety_blocks_corr: -0.31\n      gru_lead_vs_last_safety_streak_length_corr: 0.23\n      gru_failure_mode_1_pct: 0.45\n      gru_failure_mode_2_pct: 0.35\n      gru_failure_mode_3_pct: 0.20\n    result_rationale: >\n      The oracle achieves +31 step lead time because it has access to the FUTURE label sequence —\n      it can detect the last safety label before it happens. The oracle's lead time is strongly\n      correlated (r=0.87) with the length of the last safety reasoning streak, because longer\n      final streaks give more \"runway\" for early detection using the K=5 consecutive-absence\n      criterion. GRU's weak correlation (r=0.23) with streak length reveals the fundamental gap:\n      hidden states encode current safety state (is the model currently safety-reasoning?) but\n      don't reliably predict how long the current safety block will continue. GRU failure modes:\n      (1) 45% — fails to fire at all (safety signal never crosses threshold); (2) 35% — fires\n      too late (after CP, during harmful generation); (3) 20% — fires at correct time but on\n      wrong traces (false positives that happen to align).\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S4: Oracle lead time vs last safety streak length (scatter + regression line, r=0.87). GRU lead time shows no clear relationship (r=0.23).\"\n      - \"Figure S5: GRU failure mode decomposition pie chart with example traces for each mode.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The oracle decomposition reveals that reliable pre-textual detection requires predicting\n      safety reasoning TRAJECTORY, not just current state. This is the fundamental architectural\n      gap that BOCPD addresses from a different angle — instead of predicting future trajectory,\n      BOCPD detects distributional CHANGE in the probe score sequence, which is a backwards-looking\n      (causal) operation that GRU should theoretically also perform but fails to learn reliably.\n      This analysis belongs in the Discussion section and motivates future work on trajectory\n      prediction architectures.\n\n  - name: \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B, 4th model)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: >\n      Complete 2×2 architecture×scale matrix by adding OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base).\n      Full pipeline: layer sweep + static + temporal + GRU.\n    expected_results:\n      openthinker_best_layer: 14\n      openthinker_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.50\n      openthinker_num_layers: 28\n      openthinker_hidden_dim: 3584\n      openthinker_static_crossing: 0.419\n      openthinker_temporal_crossing: 0.721\n      openthinker_temporal_lead_mean: -0.4\n      openthinker_gru_lead_mean: 3.8\n      openthinker_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.37\n      scale_effect_qwen_7b_vs_32b_temporal: \"-2.3pp\"\n      arch_effect_7b_llama_vs_qwen_temporal: \"-4.6pp\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      OpenThinker-7B (28 layers, 3584-d) completes the 2×2 matrix. Qwen2.5-7B has fewer layers\n      than Llama-3.1-8B (28 vs 32) and smaller hidden dim (3584 vs 4096), which slightly reduces\n      the probe's capacity — hence temporal crossing is slightly lower than r1-8b (0.721 vs 0.767).\n      The scale effect within Qwen family is small (-2.3pp, 7B vs 32B) confirming that temporal\n      advantage is not scale-dependent. The architecture effect at matched scale (Llama-8B vs\n      Qwen-7B) is moderate (-4.6pp) and may reflect differences in how the two architectures\n      structure safety reasoning during CoT (Llama's longer CoT may produce cleaner safety blocks).\n      Best layer at 50% depth (14/28) is consistent with the ~44-50% range seen across other models.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S4: 2×2 Architecture × Scale Matrix\"\n        columns: [family, scale, model, static_crossing, temporal_crossing, delta, gru_lead_mean]\n        rows:\n          - [Llama-3.1, 8B, r1-8b, 0.465, 0.767, \"+30.2pp\", 4.7]\n          - [Qwen2.5, 7B, OpenThinker-7B, 0.419, 0.721, \"+30.2pp\", 3.8]\n          - [Qwen2.5, 32B, r1-32b, 0.395, 0.791, \"+39.6pp\", 5.2]\n          - [Qwen2.5, 32B, QwQ-32B, 0.372, 0.744, \"+37.2pp\", 3.9]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S6: 2×2 matrix visualization — temporal crossing rate by architecture (x) and scale (y). Both dimensions show modest effects; temporal advantage (+30-40pp) dominates.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 2×2 matrix confirms that the temporal advantage is the dominant factor (+30-40pp),\n      dwarfing both scale effects (<3pp) and architecture effects (<5pp). This is the strongest\n      possible evidence for Claim 2: temporal context matters more than model family or scale.\n\n  - name: \"GRU/Attention probe on 32B models\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: >\n      GRU h=256 multi-layer and Attention probe on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Test whether\n      more data/capacity helps on 32B, or temporal LR remains optimal.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_gru_crossing: 0.767\n      r1_32b_gru_acc: 0.849\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_mean: 5.2\n      r1_32b_attention_crossing: 0.744\n      r1_32b_attention_acc: 0.841\n      qwq_32b_gru_crossing: 0.721\n      qwq_32b_gru_acc: 0.831\n      qwq_32b_attention_crossing: 0.698\n      delta_gru_vs_temporal_lr_crossing: \"≤ +0pp\"\n      gru_vs_lr_param_ratio: \"1623k vs 77k\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      GRU and attention probes achieve comparable or slightly lower crossing rates than temporal LR\n      on 32B models. With only ~125 training traces, the more complex models (1.6M params for GRU\n      vs 77k for temporal LR) cannot leverage their additional capacity — they overfit to training\n      trace idiosyncrasies rather than learning more generalizable patterns. The GRU's advantage\n      is in lead time (mean +5.2 vs LR's +0.3) because it can model the sequential dynamics of\n      probe score evolution, but this comes at the cost of higher variance and unreliable detection\n      (median 0). Temporal LR's simplicity IS its strength: fewer parameters, no sequential\n      dependencies, deterministic inference. This confirms the \"simplicity is optimal for coverage\"\n      story — complex models might help with timing (lead time) but don't improve the fundamental\n      coverage metric.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S5: Complex Probes vs Temporal LR on 32B\"\n        columns: [model, probe, params, crossing, acc, lead_mean, lead_median]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-32b, Temporal LR W=15, \"77k\", 0.791, 0.854, 0.3, 0]\n          - [r1-32b, GRU h=256, \"1623k\", 0.767, 0.849, 5.2, 0]\n          - [r1-32b, Attention, \"890k\", 0.744, 0.841, 2.8, 0]\n          - [QwQ-32B, Temporal LR W=15, \"77k\", 0.744, 0.839, -0.8, 0]\n          - [QwQ-32B, GRU h=256, \"1623k\", 0.721, 0.831, 3.9, 0]\n          - [QwQ-32B, Attention, \"890k\", 0.698, 0.823, 1.6, 0]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S7: Probe complexity vs coverage (crossing rate) on 32B models. Flat or slightly declining curve — more parameters don't help coverage.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Complex probes don't outperform temporal LR on coverage, confirming that the bottleneck is\n      data (125 traces) not model capacity. The GRU's lead time advantage motivates its probe\n      scores as INPUT to BOCPD, creating a two-stage pipeline: GRU generates probe scores with\n      the best available temporal dynamics, BOCPD detects change points in those scores.\n\n  - name: \"Text-based baseline (sentence-BERT, all models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-013\"\n    description: >\n      Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) embeddings + temporal LR W=15 on CoT text.\n      Extended to 32B models. Compare coverage/lead/accuracy to hidden-state probes.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_text_static_coverage: 0.558\n      r1_32b_text_static_acc: 0.649\n      r1_32b_text_temporal_coverage: 0.628\n      r1_32b_text_temporal_acc: 0.667\n      r1_32b_text_temporal_auroc: 0.742\n      qwq_32b_text_static_coverage: 0.535\n      qwq_32b_text_static_acc: 0.638\n      qwq_32b_text_temporal_coverage: 0.605\n      qwq_32b_text_temporal_acc: 0.654\n      hs_vs_text_acc_gap_r1_32b: 0.187\n      hs_vs_text_acc_gap_qwq_32b: 0.172\n      hs_vs_text_coverage_gap_r1_32b: 0.163\n      hs_vs_text_coverage_gap_qwq_32b: 0.139\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier requires hs_vs_text_acc_gap_32b in [0.12, 0.20]. Text-based probes achieve\n      moderate performance (60-65% coverage) because CoT text does contain safety-relevant\n      lexical cues — words like \"refuse,\" \"harmful,\" \"ethical\" appear in safety reasoning blocks.\n      However, hidden-state probes outperform by +15-19pp accuracy because: (1) hidden states\n      encode safety intent BEFORE it manifests in text (pre-textual signal), (2) hidden states\n      capture the model's internal commitment state, not just surface-level word choice, (3)\n      sentence-BERT is a generic embedding not tuned for safety semantics. The gap is larger on\n      32B models because larger models have richer internal representations that diverge more\n      from surface text — the safety commitment decision is made deeper in the representation\n      hierarchy. This cleanly justifies hidden-state monitoring over cheap text-based alternatives.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 9: Hidden-State vs Text-Based Probes Across Models\"\n        columns: [model, modality, temporal_coverage, temporal_acc, temporal_auroc, acc_gap_vs_text]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, Hidden-state, 0.767, 0.848, 0.872, \"+18.0pp\"]\n          - [r1-8b, Text (SBERT), 0.651, 0.678, 0.759, \"—\"]\n          - [r1-32b, Hidden-state, 0.791, 0.854, 0.891, \"+18.7pp\"]\n          - [r1-32b, Text (SBERT), 0.628, 0.667, 0.742, \"—\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, Hidden-state, 0.744, 0.839, 0.876, \"+17.2pp\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, Text (SBERT), 0.605, 0.654, 0.731, \"—\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 12: Side-by-side ROC curves for hidden-state vs text probes on all 3 models. Hidden-state curves consistently above text curves, with largest gap in the high-specificity (low FPR) region.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The consistent +15-19pp accuracy advantage of hidden-state probes over text-based alternatives\n      is the clearest evidence that hidden-state monitoring provides unique value. The gap is largest\n      in the high-specificity regime (FPR < 0.05), which is exactly where a deployment system would\n      operate — text probes degrade more at strict thresholds because they rely on surface cues that\n      are noisier. This directly addresses the critical reviewer concern: hidden-state monitoring is\n      not redundant with text monitoring.\n\n  - name: \"BOCPD change-point detection on probe scores\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-014\"\n    description: >\n      Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (Adams & MacKay 2007) applied to per-step probe\n      confidence score sequences. Also test CUSUM as simpler alternative. Evaluated on all models.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_bocpd_crossing_rate: 0.721\n      r1_8b_bocpd_lead_mean: 12.4\n      r1_8b_bocpd_lead_median: 7\n      r1_8b_bocpd_lead_std: 11.2\n      r1_8b_bocpd_positive_lead_rate: 0.67\n      r1_32b_bocpd_crossing_rate: 0.698\n      r1_32b_bocpd_lead_mean: 14.1\n      r1_32b_bocpd_lead_median: 9\n      r1_32b_bocpd_positive_lead_rate: 0.71\n      qwq_32b_bocpd_crossing_rate: 0.674\n      qwq_32b_bocpd_lead_mean: 10.8\n      qwq_32b_bocpd_lead_median: 5\n      qwq_32b_bocpd_positive_lead_rate: 0.62\n      cusum_lead_mean: 8.3\n      cusum_lead_median: 4\n      cusum_positive_lead_rate: 0.58\n      bocpd_vs_gru_lead_improvement: \"+7-9 steps mean\"\n      bocpd_vs_gru_positive_lead_improvement: \"+26-30pp\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier requires bocpd_lead_mean [8, 20], bocpd_lead_median [3, 12], bocpd_positive_lead_rate\n      [0.55, 0.8]. BOCPD succeeds where GRU fails because it solves a fundamentally different problem:\n      instead of predicting future safety trajectory from hidden states (what GRU attempts), BOCPD\n      detects distributional SHIFTS in the probe score time series. The probe score sequence exhibits\n      a clear regime change around CP — from high-variance oscillation (safety reasoning present) to\n      monotonic low values (safety reasoning absent). BOCPD is designed exactly for this: detecting\n      when the generative process of a time series changes. The Bayesian framework provides natural\n      uncertainty quantification, and the hazard function prior can be calibrated to the expected\n      CP frequency (~1 per trace). BOCPD achieves positive median lead time (5-9 steps) because\n      the distributional shift begins BEFORE the textual CP — the probe scores start declining as\n      the model's hidden states shift toward commitment even while safety-labeled text is still\n      being generated. This is the \"pre-textual\" signal that justifies hidden-state monitoring.\n      CUSUM achieves slightly lower performance because it uses fixed thresholds rather than Bayesian\n      updating, but still outperforms GRU. The ~5pp crossing rate reduction vs temporal LR (0.67-0.72\n      vs 0.74-0.79) is the cost of requiring a detectable distributional shift — traces where the\n      transition is gradual (no sharp change point) are missed by BOCPD but caught by LR's sliding\n      window.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 10: BOCPD vs GRU vs Temporal LR — Lead Time Comparison\"\n        columns: [model, method, crossing_rate, lead_mean, lead_median, lead_std, positive_lead_rate]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, Temporal LR, 0.767, -1.09, 0, 8.2, \"42%\"]\n          - [r1-8b, GRU multi-7L, 0.767, 4.7, 0, 13.0, \"39%\"]\n          - [r1-8b, BOCPD, 0.721, 12.4, 7, 11.2, \"67%\"]\n          - [r1-8b, CUSUM, 0.698, 8.3, 4, 10.5, \"58%\"]\n          - [r1-32b, Temporal LR, 0.791, 0.3, 0, 8.8, \"44%\"]\n          - [r1-32b, GRU multi-7L, 0.744, 5.2, 0, 14.8, \"41%\"]\n          - [r1-32b, BOCPD, 0.698, 14.1, 9, 12.0, \"71%\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, Temporal LR, 0.744, -0.8, 0, 7.9, \"40%\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, GRU multi-7L, 0.698, 3.9, 0, 12.3, \"36%\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, BOCPD, 0.674, 10.8, 5, 10.8, \"62%\"]\n          - [r1-8b, Oracle, 1.000, 31.0, 29, 15.2, \"100%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 13: Lead time violin plots for GRU vs BOCPD vs Oracle across all models. BOCPD distributions are unimodal and shifted positive; GRU distributions are bimodal with spike at 0.\"\n      - \"Figure 14: BOCPD run-length posterior visualization on 3 example traces — shows change-point probability spiking 5-15 steps before textual CP.\"\n      - \"Figure 15: Coverage-lead time Pareto frontier. Temporal LR dominates coverage axis; BOCPD dominates lead time axis; no single method dominates both.\"\n    analysis: >\n      BOCPD is the breakthrough result in the best tier. It achieves the paper's upgraded Claim 3:\n      reliable pre-textual detection IS possible with the right method. The key insight is that\n      change-point detection on probe scores is complementary to probe accuracy — even an imperfect\n      probe produces score sequences with detectable regime changes. BOCPD's median lead time of\n      5-9 steps means typical detection occurs 5-9 reasoning steps before the textual commitment\n      point, providing a meaningful intervention window. The positive lead rate of 62-71% means\n      the majority of detected traces achieve genuine early detection, versus GRU's 36-41%.\n      This constitutes a genuine methods contribution: applying BOCPD to probe score sequences\n      is novel in the safety detection literature and demonstrably effective. The coverage-lead\n      time tradeoff (BOCPD sacrifices ~5pp coverage for +8-10 step lead time vs temporal LR)\n      suggests a two-stage deployment: temporal LR for high-coverage monitoring, BOCPD for\n      early-warning alerting.\n\n  - name: \"False positive analysis on safe traces\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-015\"\n    description: >\n      Run all probes (static LR, temporal LR, GRU, BOCPD) on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5).\n      Report FPR at operating thresholds, precision-recall curves, deployment utility analysis.\n    expected_results:\n      static_lr_fpr: 0.082\n      temporal_lr_fpr: 0.054\n      gru_fpr: 0.071\n      bocpd_fpr: 0.038\n      temporal_lr_precision_at_80_recall: 0.91\n      temporal_lr_precision_at_90_recall: 0.84\n      bocpd_precision_at_80_recall: 0.94\n      bocpd_precision_at_90_recall: 0.87\n      num_safe_traces: 48\n      safe_trace_max_consecutive_alerts: 2\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier requires fpr_at_operating_threshold in [0.02, 0.10]. Safe traces (final_judgment=0.5)\n      contain normal reasoning without self-jailbreak — the model considers the request, may briefly\n      engage safety reasoning, but ultimately provides a safe response. Temporal LR achieves low FPR\n      (5.4%) because the characteristic safety density decline pattern — the monotonic decrease in\n      safety-labeled steps within the window — does not occur in safe traces. Safe traces may\n      show intermittent safety reasoning, but it doesn't PERMANENTLY cease. BOCPD has the lowest\n      FPR (3.8%) because its change-point criterion requires a sustained distributional shift, not\n      just a momentary probe score dip — brief fluctuations in safe traces don't trigger the\n      run-length posterior. Static LR has higher FPR (8.2%) because individual steps in safe traces\n      can incidentally resemble post-CP jailbreak steps (e.g., when the model reasons about the\n      task itself without explicit safety language). GRU's FPR (7.1%) is moderate — it sometimes\n      fires on safe traces where the probe score sequence happens to resemble a commitment trajectory\n      by chance. The safe_trace_max_consecutive_alerts of 2 means that even when false positives\n      occur, they don't persist — a simple debounce (require 3+ consecutive alerts) would reduce\n      effective FPR near zero.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 11: False Positive Analysis on Safe Traces\"\n        columns: [probe, fpr_at_operating_threshold, precision_at_80_recall, precision_at_90_recall, max_consecutive_fp]\n        rows:\n          - [Static LR, 0.082, 0.87, 0.78, 3]\n          - [Temporal LR W=15, 0.054, 0.91, 0.84, 2]\n          - [GRU multi-7L, 0.071, 0.89, 0.81, 2]\n          - [BOCPD, 0.038, 0.94, 0.87, 1]\n          - [CUSUM, 0.045, 0.92, 0.85, 2]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 16: Precision-recall curves for all probes. BOCPD and temporal LR dominate — high precision maintained even at 90% recall.\"\n      - \"Figure 17: Probe score time series on 4 example safe traces vs 4 jailbreak traces. Safe traces show stable oscillation; jailbreak traces show clear transition to sustained low scores.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Low FPR across all probes confirms that coverage metrics are deployment-meaningful — high\n      coverage is not achieved by simply alerting on everything. BOCPD's lowest FPR (3.8%) combined\n      with its lead time advantage makes it the recommended deployment method for early warning.\n      Temporal LR's slightly higher FPR (5.4%) but better coverage (0.77 vs 0.72) makes it the\n      recommended method for comprehensive monitoring. The two methods serve complementary deployment\n      roles. The precision-at-90%-recall of 0.84-0.87 means that in deployment, fewer than 1 in 6\n      alerts would be false positives, which is operationally acceptable for a safety monitoring system.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3400,"cache_read_input_tokens":24173,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3400},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"47a9f493-e1d7-4c88-83ab-6d41811d63f4","uuid":"41708cfe-fac3-45d8-82dc-9ccedcef3ca5"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":386136,"duration_api_ms":386072,"num_turns":3,"result":"```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  All 3 claims hold as framed. Claim 1 (static fails) generalizes cross-architecture: 32B Qwen-based models show\n  static crossing rates 35-42%, matching the 8B Llama pattern. Claim 2 (temporal saturates oracle) confirmed on\n  4 models, 2 families, 2 scales — temporal LR W=15 achieves 73-82% crossing rate across all models. Claim 3\n  upgraded: BOCPD achieves reliable pre-textual detection (median lead +7, >60% positive lead rate) where GRU\n  failed, constituting a genuine methods contribution. FPR <8% on safe traces confirms deployment utility.\n  Text baseline cleanly demonstrates hidden-state unique value (+15pp accuracy on 32B). Cross-model transfer\n  retains >65% of within-family performance, enabling zero-shot deployment story. Paper submits to EMNLP Main\n  with strong empirical + methods narrative: 'temporal context is necessary AND sufficient for coverage;\n  change-point detection unlocks timing.'\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Cross-architecture layer sweep + static probe (32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: >\n      Extract hidden states from all 64 layers of r1-32b and QwQ-32B on HarmThoughts traces.\n      Layer sweep identifies best probing layer. Static LR evaluated at best layer.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_best_layer_idx: 28\n      r1_32b_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.44\n      r1_32b_static_acc: 0.823\n      r1_32b_static_f1: 0.671\n      r1_32b_static_crossing_rate: 0.395\n      r1_32b_static_lead_mean: -1.4\n      r1_32b_fisher_orig: 18.3\n      r1_32b_fisher_pca3: 1.02\n      r1_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc: 0.148\n      qwq_32b_best_layer_idx: 30\n      qwq_32b_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.47\n      qwq_32b_static_acc: 0.808\n      qwq_32b_static_f1: 0.643\n      qwq_32b_static_crossing_rate: 0.372\n      qwq_32b_static_lead_mean: -2.1\n      qwq_32b_fisher_orig: 17.6\n      qwq_32b_fisher_pca3: 0.96\n      qwq_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc: 0.155\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier requires static crossing rates in [0.3, 0.5] on both 32B models — validating that\n      static probes fail cross-architecture. r1-32b (Qwen2.5-32B base, same family as QwQ) should\n      show slightly higher accuracy than QwQ due to distillation from full R1, but both fail the\n      crossing criterion. Best layer at ~44-47% depth is consistent with the 8B finding (layer 14/32 = 44%):\n      middle layers encode task-relevant abstractions while early layers are too syntactic and late layers\n      are too committed to output distribution. Fisher ratio remains high in full 5120-d space (~17-18)\n      but collapses in PCA-3d (~1.0) because the safety signal is distributed across hundreds of minor\n      principal components — this is the geometric failure mode that makes Mahalanobis/TrajGuard-style\n      detectors fail. The 5120-d hidden states of 32B models have even more capacity to spread signal\n      thinly, so PCA variance explained may be slightly lower than 8B's 16.6%.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2: Static Probe Results Across Architectures\"\n        columns: [model, arch_family, scale, best_layer, best_layer_depth, static_acc, static_crossing, static_lead_mean, fisher_full, fisher_pca3]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, Llama-3.1, 8B, 14, \"44%\", 0.817, 0.465, -2.0, 17.9, 0.93]\n          - [r1-32b, Qwen2.5, 32B, 28, \"44%\", 0.823, 0.395, -1.4, 18.3, 1.02]\n          - [QwQ-32B, Qwen2.5, 32B, 30, \"47%\", 0.808, 0.372, -2.1, 17.6, 0.96]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 3: Layer sweep accuracy curves for r1-32b and QwQ-32B (64 layers). Both show inverted-U with peak at 44-47% depth, mirroring r1-8b's layer 14 (44%). Dashed line shows r1-8b reference.\"\n      - \"Figure 4: PCA scatter (3D) of hidden states at best layer for all 3 models. Safe (blue) and jailbreak-pre-CP (orange) heavily overlap in top-3 PCs despite Fisher ratio >17 in full space.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Static probes achieve reasonable per-step accuracy (80-82%) but fail the deployment-critical\n      crossing rate metric (<50% on all models). This confirms Claim 1 cross-architecture: the\n      safety commitment signal exists in hidden states but is geometrically inaccessible to\n      per-step classifiers. The consistency of best-layer depth (~44%) across architectures suggests\n      a universal property of transformer safety representations, not an artifact of Llama architecture.\n\n  - name: \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: >\n      Temporal LR W=15 evaluated on r1-32b and QwQ-32B at best layers from exp-001.\n      Validates +30pp coverage improvement cross-architecture.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_temporal_acc: 0.854\n      r1_32b_temporal_crossing_rate: 0.791\n      r1_32b_temporal_lead_mean: 0.3\n      r1_32b_temporal_auroc: 0.891\n      r1_32b_delta_over_static_crossing: \"+39.6pp\"\n      qwq_32b_temporal_acc: 0.839\n      qwq_32b_temporal_crossing_rate: 0.744\n      qwq_32b_temporal_lead_mean: -0.8\n      qwq_32b_temporal_auroc: 0.876\n      qwq_32b_delta_over_static_crossing: \"+37.2pp\"\n      cross_arch_temporal_crossing_mean: 0.778\n      cross_arch_static_crossing_mean: 0.411\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier requires cross_arch_temporal_crossing_rate in [0.7, 0.85]. The temporal window\n      captures \"safety reasoning density decline\" — a pattern where the fraction of safety-labeled\n      steps within a 15-step window drops monotonically toward zero near CP. This pattern is a\n      property of the self-jailbreak phenomenon itself, not of any specific architecture, so it\n      should replicate on Qwen-based models. r1-32b slightly outperforms QwQ because R1 distillation\n      may produce more structured safety reasoning patterns. Both show +30-40pp improvement over\n      static, confirming temporal context is the key missing ingredient. Lead times cluster near zero\n      (range [-2, 2]) because temporal LR detects the crossing AT the commitment point, not before it —\n      it recognizes the pattern as it completes, not in advance. This is expected and consistent with\n      8B results (-1.09).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Temporal vs Static Probe Coverage Across Architectures\"\n        columns: [model, static_crossing, temporal_crossing, delta, temporal_lead_mean, temporal_auroc]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, 0.465, 0.767, \"+30.2pp\", -1.09, 0.872]\n          - [r1-32b, 0.395, 0.791, \"+39.6pp\", 0.3, 0.891]\n          - [QwQ-32B, 0.372, 0.744, \"+37.2pp\", -0.8, 0.876]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 5: Coverage (crossing rate) bar chart — static vs temporal — across 3 models. Temporal consistently 70-80%, static consistently 37-47%. Error bars from 5-fold CV.\"\n      - \"Figure 6: Detection delay CDF for temporal LR on all 3 models. Median delay ~1-2 steps from CP. Curves nearly overlap, confirming cross-architecture consistency.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The +30-40pp temporal advantage replicates robustly across architectures and scales. The\n      consistency (all 3 models in [0.74, 0.79] range) strongly supports Claim 2. Temporal LR\n      achieves near-oracle coverage (oracle = 76.7% on 8B; 32B oracles may be slightly different\n      due to different trace characteristics). The key insight is that W=15 is sufficient to\n      capture the safety density decline regardless of model — the phenomenon is architecture-agnostic.\n\n  - name: \"Cross-model transfer (Llama↔Qwen)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: >\n      Train probe on r1-8b (Llama-3.1, 4096-d), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5, 5120-d)\n      and vice versa. Learned projection head (4096→5120 or 5120→4096). Both static and\n      temporal probes evaluated in transfer setting.\n    expected_results:\n      llama_to_r1_32b_static_crossing: 0.267\n      llama_to_r1_32b_temporal_crossing: 0.523\n      llama_to_qwq_static_crossing: 0.244\n      llama_to_qwq_temporal_crossing: 0.488\n      qwen_to_llama_static_crossing: 0.302\n      qwen_to_llama_temporal_crossing: 0.558\n      cross_transfer_coverage_retention_temporal: 0.68\n      cross_transfer_coverage_retention_static: 0.62\n      within_family_qwen_transfer_crossing: 0.698\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier requires cross_transfer_coverage_retention in [0.6, 0.8]. Transfer between\n      architectures with different hidden dimensions (4096 vs 5120) requires a learned projection,\n      which loses some representational fidelity. However, the safety commitment signal appears to\n      occupy a relatively low-dimensional subspace despite being spread across many PCs — meaning\n      a linear projection can capture the essential structure. Temporal probes transfer better than\n      static (68% vs 62% retention) because the temporal pattern (density decline over 15 steps)\n      is more architecture-invariant than the exact per-step feature geometry. Within-family transfer\n      (r1-32b → QwQ, both Qwen2.5 base) achieves higher retention (~70%) because the representation\n      spaces share more structure from the common pretraining. Qwen→Llama transfers slightly better\n      than Llama→Qwen because training on 32B's richer representations produces a more generalizable\n      probe.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Cross-Architecture Transfer Results\"\n        columns: [train_model, test_model, same_family, static_crossing, temporal_crossing, retention_vs_within]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b (Llama), r1-32b (Qwen), No, 0.267, 0.523, \"66%\"]\n          - [r1-8b (Llama), QwQ-32B (Qwen), No, 0.244, 0.488, \"66%\"]\n          - [r1-32b (Qwen), r1-8b (Llama), No, 0.302, 0.558, \"73%\"]\n          - [r1-32b (Qwen), QwQ-32B (Qwen), Yes, 0.349, 0.698, \"88%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 7: Transfer coverage matrix heatmap (train model × test model) for temporal probes. Diagonal (within-model) is darkest, within-family is medium, cross-family lightest but still >0.48.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Cross-architecture transfer retains 62-73% of within-family performance, supporting the\n      claim that safety commitment representations share cross-architecture structure. The temporal\n      probe's superior transfer (68% vs 62% for static) further validates that temporal patterns\n      are the more universal signal. Within-family transfer (88% retention) suggests deployment\n      could use a single Qwen-family probe for multiple Qwen-based reasoning models.\n\n  - name: \"Lead time analysis across models (32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: >\n      GRU multi-layer lead time analysis on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Report full lead time\n      distributions per model.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_mean: 5.2\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_median: 0\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_std: 14.8\n      r1_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.41\n      r1_32b_gru_crossing_rate: 0.744\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean: 3.9\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_median: 0\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_std: 12.3\n      qwq_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.36\n      qwq_32b_gru_crossing_rate: 0.698\n      cross_model_lead_mean_range: \"[3.9, 5.2]\"\n      cross_model_positive_lead_rate_range: \"[0.36, 0.41]\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      GRU lead time on 32B models should be similar to 8B (+4.7 mean, median 0, 39% positive lead).\n      The fundamental limitation — hidden states encode current safety state well but poorly predict\n      future safety trajectory — is a property of the safety commitment phenomenon, not of model\n      scale. Larger models have longer traces on average, which slightly helps GRU accumulate context\n      (r1-32b lead_mean 5.2 vs 8B's 4.7), but the median remains 0 and positive lead rate stays below\n      45%. This confirms the gap is architectural (sequential models can't reliably anticipate CP)\n      rather than capacity-limited. The consistency across 3 models (mean lead 3.9-5.2, all medians 0)\n      strengthens the \"fundamental limitation\" narrative while motivating BOCPD as the solution.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: GRU Lead Time Distributions Across Models\"\n        columns: [model, crossing_rate, lead_mean, lead_median, lead_std, positive_lead_rate, n_detected]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, 0.767, 4.7, 0, 13.0, \"39% (13/33)\", 33]\n          - [r1-32b, 0.744, 5.2, 0, 14.8, \"41% (14/34)\", 34]\n          - [QwQ-32B, 0.698, 3.9, 0, 12.3, \"36% (11/31)\", 31]\n          - [Oracle (r1-8b), 1.000, 31.0, 29, 15.2, \"100%\", 43]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 8: Lead time distribution histograms (violin plots) for GRU across 3 models + oracle. All GRU distributions are bimodal: a spike at 0 and a long positive tail. Oracle is unimodal positive.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The GRU lead time pattern replicates cross-architecture: positive mean (3.9-5.2) but median 0\n      and <41% positive lead rate. This strongly motivates BOCPD (exp-014) as a post-hoc method that\n      can achieve reliable positive lead time by detecting distributional shifts in probe score\n      sequences rather than trying to predict future safety trajectory from hidden states directly.\n\n  - name: \"TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline (all models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: >\n      Mahalanobis distance baseline on r1-32b and QwQ-32B (r1-8b already done). Uses safe traces\n      to estimate covariance, computes MD per step. PCA-64 + percentile thresholds.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_best_crossing_rate: 0.279\n      r1_32b_best_lead_mean: 18.2\n      r1_32b_safe_md_mean: 7.12\n      r1_32b_jb_pre_md_mean: 7.19\n      r1_32b_jb_post_md_mean: 7.08\n      r1_32b_delta_max_rate: 0.019\n      qwq_32b_best_crossing_rate: 0.256\n      qwq_32b_best_lead_mean: 15.7\n      qwq_32b_safe_md_mean: 6.89\n      qwq_32b_jb_pre_md_mean: 6.94\n      qwq_32b_jb_post_md_mean: 6.82\n      qwq_32b_delta_max_rate: 0.017\n    result_rationale: >\n      TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis distance fundamentally fails because safety commitment signal is\n      distributed across minor principal components (Fisher drops from ~18→1 in PCA-3d). MD measures\n      deviation from the \"safe\" covariance structure, but jailbreak pre-CP and post-CP hidden states\n      have nearly identical distances from the safe centroid — the signal is in the DIRECTION of change\n      over time (temporal), not in the POSITION in representation space (geometric). 32B models have\n      higher-dimensional hidden states (5120-d) with even more capacity to spread signal thinly,\n      so MD discrimination is slightly worse than 8B. The ~0.02 delta between safe and jailbreak MD\n      means is negligible relative to the within-class variance. This cleanly validates \"existing\n      geometric detectors fail\" across architectures.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: Mahalanobis Distance Baseline Across Models\"\n        columns: [model, safe_md_mean, jb_pre_md, jb_post_md, delta_max, best_crossing_rate, best_config]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, 6.66, 6.73, 6.64, 0.023, 0.302, pca_64_p75]\n          - [r1-32b, 7.12, 7.19, 7.08, 0.019, 0.279, pca_64_p75]\n          - [QwQ-32B, 6.89, 6.94, 6.82, 0.017, 0.256, pca_64_p80]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 9: MD distribution overlays (safe vs jb_pre vs jb_post) for all 3 models. Near-complete overlap in all cases. Inset shows the tiny mean differences (~0.02) are statistically insignificant.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Mahalanobis distance fails uniformly across architectures, confirming that geometric methods\n      designed for instruction-tuned adversarial inputs (TrajGuard, SIREN) cannot detect reasoning-model\n      self-jailbreak. The failure mode is principled: safety commitment signal lives in minor PCs that\n      contribute negligibly to the covariance-weighted distance. This justifies temporal methods as\n      architecturally necessary, not just empirically better.\n\n  - name: \"Bootstrap CIs + threshold-independent metrics (all models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: >\n      1000 bootstrap resamples for 95% CIs on all key metrics across all models. Paired significance\n      tests. AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF, per-fold CV variance. Includes 32B models.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_temporal_acc_ci: \"[0.798, 0.901]\"\n      r1_32b_temporal_crossing_ci: \"[0.651, 0.907]\"\n      r1_32b_temporal_lead_ci: \"[-4.2, +5.1]\"\n      qwq_32b_temporal_acc_ci: \"[0.779, 0.889]\"\n      qwq_32b_temporal_crossing_ci: \"[0.605, 0.860]\"\n      paired_temporal_vs_static_crossing_p_r1_32b: 0.003\n      paired_temporal_vs_static_crossing_p_qwq: 0.008\n      paired_temporal_vs_static_crossing_p_pooled: 0.001\n      cross_model_temporal_auroc_range: \"[0.872, 0.891]\"\n      cv_5fold_crossing_std: 0.062\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier produces tight, non-overlapping CIs for the critical comparison: temporal vs static\n      crossing rate. The pooled paired test (p=0.001) is highly significant because the +30-40pp\n      temporal advantage is large relative to the inter-trace variance. Individual model p-values\n      are significant (p<0.01) but less extreme due to smaller per-model sample sizes (~40 test\n      traces). Lead time CIs still cross zero because median lead is 0 — this is expected and\n      doesn't weaken the paper because lead time improvement is Claim 3's domain (addressed by\n      BOCPD). 5-fold CV std of 0.062 on crossing rate confirms low variance. AUROC 0.87-0.89\n      across models is strong for a deployment-relevant metric.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: Bootstrap 95% CIs for Key Metrics (All Models)\"\n        columns: [model, metric, point_estimate, ci_lower, ci_upper]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, temporal_crossing, 0.767, 0.628, 0.884]\n          - [r1-8b, temporal_lead, -1.09, -6.2, 4.8]\n          - [r1-32b, temporal_crossing, 0.791, 0.651, 0.907]\n          - [r1-32b, temporal_lead, 0.3, -4.2, 5.1]\n          - [QwQ-32B, temporal_crossing, 0.744, 0.605, 0.860]\n          - [QwQ-32B, temporal_lead, -0.8, -5.5, 4.3]\n      - name: \"Table 8: Paired Significance Tests (Temporal vs Static Crossing Rate)\"\n        columns: [comparison, model, p_value, significant_at_005]\n        rows:\n          - [temporal vs static, r1-8b, 0.005, Yes]\n          - [temporal vs static, r1-32b, 0.003, Yes]\n          - [temporal vs static, QwQ-32B, 0.008, Yes]\n          - [temporal vs static, pooled, 0.001, Yes]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 10: Forest plot of temporal crossing rate with 95% bootstrap CIs for all 3 models + pooled estimate. All CIs above 0.60, pooled estimate 0.77 [0.70, 0.84].\"\n      - \"Figure 11: PR curves for static, temporal, GRU across all models. Temporal dominates static at all recall levels. GRU matches temporal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Bootstrap analysis confirms the temporal advantage is statistically significant (pooled p=0.001)\n      and reproducible across models. The CIs are wide enough to reflect the limited test set sizes\n      (~40 traces) but narrow enough that temporal and static distributions don't overlap at the point\n      estimate level. This addresses the reviewer concern about small sample sizes by showing the\n      effect size is large enough to survive resampling.\n\n  - name: \"Window size ablation (W=1..25, all models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: >\n      Temporal LR with W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all 3 models. Identify optimal W and\n      characterize diminishing returns.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_w1_crossing: 0.465\n      r1_8b_w3_crossing: 0.535\n      r1_8b_w5_crossing: 0.605\n      r1_8b_w10_crossing: 0.698\n      r1_8b_w15_crossing: 0.767\n      r1_8b_w20_crossing: 0.767\n      r1_8b_w25_crossing: 0.744\n      r1_32b_w15_crossing: 0.791\n      r1_32b_w20_crossing: 0.791\n      r1_32b_w25_crossing: 0.767\n      qwq_32b_w15_crossing: 0.744\n      qwq_32b_w20_crossing: 0.744\n      optimal_w_all_models: 15\n      saturation_w: 15\n    result_rationale: >\n      W=1 reduces to a static probe (no temporal context) so crossing rate matches static baseline.\n      Coverage increases monotonically from W=1 to W=15 as the window captures progressively more\n      of the safety density decline pattern. At W=15, the window spans enough steps to observe the\n      critical transition from safety-present to safety-absent reasoning. W=20 shows no improvement\n      (saturation) because the additional 5 steps of history add noise without new signal — the\n      commitment process completes within ~15 steps. W=25 may slightly degrade due to including\n      irrelevant early-trace context that dilutes the near-CP signal. This saturation at W=15 aligns\n      with the oracle analysis showing mean safety streak length of 14.6 steps — the window needs\n      to be at least as wide as the typical safety reasoning block to detect its cessation.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S1: Window Size Ablation (Crossing Rate)\"\n        columns: [W, r1-8b, r1-32b, QwQ-32B, mean]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 0.465, 0.395, 0.372, 0.411]\n          - [3, 0.535, 0.488, 0.442, 0.488]\n          - [5, 0.605, 0.558, 0.512, 0.558]\n          - [10, 0.698, 0.698, 0.651, 0.682]\n          - [15, 0.767, 0.791, 0.744, 0.767]\n          - [20, 0.767, 0.791, 0.744, 0.767]\n          - [25, 0.744, 0.767, 0.721, 0.744]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S1: Coverage vs window size for all 3 models. Sigmoidal curve with inflection at W≈8 and saturation at W=15. Shaded region shows cross-model variance.\"\n    analysis: >\n      W=15 is optimal across all architectures, confirming the finding is not architecture-specific.\n      The saturation point (W=15) matching the mean safety streak length (14.6) is a non-trivial\n      convergence that supports the mechanistic story: temporal probes succeed because they detect\n      the cessation of a characteristic-length safety reasoning block.\n\n  - name: \"Layer selection ablation (all layers, 32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: >\n      Temporal LR W=15 evaluated at all 64 layers for r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Confirms middle-layer\n      finding generalizes and characterizes per-layer signal distribution.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_best_temporal_layer: 28\n      r1_32b_temporal_layer_range_within_2pp: \"24-34\"\n      r1_32b_early_layer_8_crossing: 0.372\n      r1_32b_late_layer_56_crossing: 0.512\n      qwq_32b_best_temporal_layer: 30\n      qwq_32b_temporal_layer_range_within_2pp: \"26-36\"\n      middle_layer_depth_pct: \"0.42-0.50\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Middle layers (~44% depth) encode the richest safety-relevant abstractions because early\n      layers represent low-level linguistic features and late layers are dominated by next-token\n      prediction logits. The temporal probe amplifies this advantage: at middle layers, the 15-step\n      window captures a coherent trajectory through the safety reasoning subspace, while at early/late\n      layers the trajectory is either too noisy (early) or too compressed (late). The broad plateau\n      (24-34 for r1-32b, ~16% of layers within 2pp of best) indicates the signal is not fragile —\n      it's accessible across a range of middle layers, which is important for deployment robustness\n      (no need to perfectly identify the single best layer).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S2: Layer Sweep for Temporal Probe (32B Models)\"\n        columns: [layer_region, r1-32b_crossing, qwq_32b_crossing]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Early (1-16)\", \"0.35-0.49\", \"0.33-0.47\"]\n          - [\"Middle (24-36)\", \"0.77-0.79\", \"0.72-0.74\"]\n          - [\"Late (48-64)\", \"0.49-0.58\", \"0.47-0.56\"]\n          - [\"Best\", \"0.791 (L28)\", \"0.744 (L30)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S2: Temporal crossing rate vs layer depth for r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Both show broad middle-layer peak. Overlaid with r1-8b (rescaled to 64 layers) showing consistent depth profile.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The middle-layer advantage generalizes cross-architecture. The broad plateau means practitioners\n      can select any layer in the 40-55% depth range without significant performance loss, making\n      the method robust to layer selection in deployment.\n\n  - name: \"16-label taxonomy supervision\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: >\n      Replace binary commitment labels with 16-category fine-grained safety taxonomy labels\n      (Type 1-4 safety reasoning subtypes) for both static and temporal probes.\n    expected_results:\n      16label_static_crossing: 0.488\n      16label_temporal_crossing: 0.791\n      16label_temporal_lead_mean: 0.8\n      binary_static_crossing: 0.465\n      binary_temporal_crossing: 0.767\n      delta_16_vs_binary_crossing: \"+2.4pp\"\n      16label_per_type_accuracy:\n        type1_refusal: 0.82\n        type2_hedging: 0.71\n        type3_ethical: 0.76\n        type4_compliance: 0.68\n    result_rationale: >\n      Fine-grained labels provide richer supervision (distinguishing refusal, hedging, ethical\n      reasoning, compliance checking) which slightly improves the probe's ability to track safety\n      reasoning density — each subtype has somewhat different hidden-state signatures. However, the\n      improvement is marginal (+2.4pp) because the binary commitment signal is already well-captured\n      by the temporal window: what matters for coverage is detecting the ABSENCE of any safety\n      reasoning, not distinguishing which type is present. The per-type accuracy variation (68-82%)\n      reveals that refusal reasoning (Type 1) is most salient in hidden states while compliance\n      checking (Type 4) is hardest to distinguish from neutral reasoning. This marginal improvement\n      doesn't justify the added annotation complexity for deployment.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S3: Binary vs 16-Label Supervision\"\n        columns: [supervision, static_crossing, temporal_crossing, temporal_lead, per_type_acc_range]\n        rows:\n          - [Binary, 0.465, 0.767, -1.09, \"N/A\"]\n          - [\"16-label\", 0.488, 0.791, 0.8, \"0.68-0.82\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S3: Confusion matrix for 16-label temporal probe showing which safety reasoning types are most/least distinguishable in hidden states.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Fine-grained labels provide marginal improvement, confirming that the binary commitment\n      signal is the primary driver of temporal probe success. This addresses the reviewer concern\n      about underusing available supervision: we tried it, it helps a little, but the binary\n      formulation captures the essential structure.\n\n  - name: \"Oracle decomposition analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: >\n      Analyze what label patterns the oracle exploits that hidden-state probes cannot.\n      Decompose oracle lead time by trace characteristics.\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_uses_future_labels: true\n      oracle_lead_vs_last_safety_streak_length_corr: 0.87\n      oracle_lead_vs_trace_length_corr: 0.42\n      oracle_lead_vs_num_safety_blocks_corr: -0.31\n      gru_lead_vs_last_safety_streak_length_corr: 0.23\n      gru_failure_mode_1_pct: 0.45\n      gru_failure_mode_2_pct: 0.35\n      gru_failure_mode_3_pct: 0.20\n    result_rationale: >\n      The oracle achieves +31 step lead time because it has access to the FUTURE label sequence —\n      it can detect the last safety label before it happens. The oracle's lead time is strongly\n      correlated (r=0.87) with the length of the last safety reasoning streak, because longer\n      final streaks give more \"runway\" for early detection using the K=5 consecutive-absence\n      criterion. GRU's weak correlation (r=0.23) with streak length reveals the fundamental gap:\n      hidden states encode current safety state (is the model currently safety-reasoning?) but\n      don't reliably predict how long the current safety block will continue. GRU failure modes:\n      (1) 45% — fails to fire at all (safety signal never crosses threshold); (2) 35% — fires\n      too late (after CP, during harmful generation); (3) 20% — fires at correct time but on\n      wrong traces (false positives that happen to align).\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S4: Oracle lead time vs last safety streak length (scatter + regression line, r=0.87). GRU lead time shows no clear relationship (r=0.23).\"\n      - \"Figure S5: GRU failure mode decomposition pie chart with example traces for each mode.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The oracle decomposition reveals that reliable pre-textual detection requires predicting\n      safety reasoning TRAJECTORY, not just current state. This is the fundamental architectural\n      gap that BOCPD addresses from a different angle — instead of predicting future trajectory,\n      BOCPD detects distributional CHANGE in the probe score sequence, which is a backwards-looking\n      (causal) operation that GRU should theoretically also perform but fails to learn reliably.\n      This analysis belongs in the Discussion section and motivates future work on trajectory\n      prediction architectures.\n\n  - name: \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B, 4th model)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: >\n      Complete 2×2 architecture×scale matrix by adding OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base).\n      Full pipeline: layer sweep + static + temporal + GRU.\n    expected_results:\n      openthinker_best_layer: 14\n      openthinker_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.50\n      openthinker_num_layers: 28\n      openthinker_hidden_dim: 3584\n      openthinker_static_crossing: 0.419\n      openthinker_temporal_crossing: 0.721\n      openthinker_temporal_lead_mean: -0.4\n      openthinker_gru_lead_mean: 3.8\n      openthinker_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.37\n      scale_effect_qwen_7b_vs_32b_temporal: \"-2.3pp\"\n      arch_effect_7b_llama_vs_qwen_temporal: \"-4.6pp\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      OpenThinker-7B (28 layers, 3584-d) completes the 2×2 matrix. Qwen2.5-7B has fewer layers\n      than Llama-3.1-8B (28 vs 32) and smaller hidden dim (3584 vs 4096), which slightly reduces\n      the probe's capacity — hence temporal crossing is slightly lower than r1-8b (0.721 vs 0.767).\n      The scale effect within Qwen family is small (-2.3pp, 7B vs 32B) confirming that temporal\n      advantage is not scale-dependent. The architecture effect at matched scale (Llama-8B vs\n      Qwen-7B) is moderate (-4.6pp) and may reflect differences in how the two architectures\n      structure safety reasoning during CoT (Llama's longer CoT may produce cleaner safety blocks).\n      Best layer at 50% depth (14/28) is consistent with the ~44-50% range seen across other models.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S4: 2×2 Architecture × Scale Matrix\"\n        columns: [family, scale, model, static_crossing, temporal_crossing, delta, gru_lead_mean]\n        rows:\n          - [Llama-3.1, 8B, r1-8b, 0.465, 0.767, \"+30.2pp\", 4.7]\n          - [Qwen2.5, 7B, OpenThinker-7B, 0.419, 0.721, \"+30.2pp\", 3.8]\n          - [Qwen2.5, 32B, r1-32b, 0.395, 0.791, \"+39.6pp\", 5.2]\n          - [Qwen2.5, 32B, QwQ-32B, 0.372, 0.744, \"+37.2pp\", 3.9]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S6: 2×2 matrix visualization — temporal crossing rate by architecture (x) and scale (y). Both dimensions show modest effects; temporal advantage (+30-40pp) dominates.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 2×2 matrix confirms that the temporal advantage is the dominant factor (+30-40pp),\n      dwarfing both scale effects (<3pp) and architecture effects (<5pp). This is the strongest\n      possible evidence for Claim 2: temporal context matters more than model family or scale.\n\n  - name: \"GRU/Attention probe on 32B models\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: >\n      GRU h=256 multi-layer and Attention probe on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Test whether\n      more data/capacity helps on 32B, or temporal LR remains optimal.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_gru_crossing: 0.767\n      r1_32b_gru_acc: 0.849\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_mean: 5.2\n      r1_32b_attention_crossing: 0.744\n      r1_32b_attention_acc: 0.841\n      qwq_32b_gru_crossing: 0.721\n      qwq_32b_gru_acc: 0.831\n      qwq_32b_attention_crossing: 0.698\n      delta_gru_vs_temporal_lr_crossing: \"≤ +0pp\"\n      gru_vs_lr_param_ratio: \"1623k vs 77k\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      GRU and attention probes achieve comparable or slightly lower crossing rates than temporal LR\n      on 32B models. With only ~125 training traces, the more complex models (1.6M params for GRU\n      vs 77k for temporal LR) cannot leverage their additional capacity — they overfit to training\n      trace idiosyncrasies rather than learning more generalizable patterns. The GRU's advantage\n      is in lead time (mean +5.2 vs LR's +0.3) because it can model the sequential dynamics of\n      probe score evolution, but this comes at the cost of higher variance and unreliable detection\n      (median 0). Temporal LR's simplicity IS its strength: fewer parameters, no sequential\n      dependencies, deterministic inference. This confirms the \"simplicity is optimal for coverage\"\n      story — complex models might help with timing (lead time) but don't improve the fundamental\n      coverage metric.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S5: Complex Probes vs Temporal LR on 32B\"\n        columns: [model, probe, params, crossing, acc, lead_mean, lead_median]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-32b, Temporal LR W=15, \"77k\", 0.791, 0.854, 0.3, 0]\n          - [r1-32b, GRU h=256, \"1623k\", 0.767, 0.849, 5.2, 0]\n          - [r1-32b, Attention, \"890k\", 0.744, 0.841, 2.8, 0]\n          - [QwQ-32B, Temporal LR W=15, \"77k\", 0.744, 0.839, -0.8, 0]\n          - [QwQ-32B, GRU h=256, \"1623k\", 0.721, 0.831, 3.9, 0]\n          - [QwQ-32B, Attention, \"890k\", 0.698, 0.823, 1.6, 0]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure S7: Probe complexity vs coverage (crossing rate) on 32B models. Flat or slightly declining curve — more parameters don't help coverage.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Complex probes don't outperform temporal LR on coverage, confirming that the bottleneck is\n      data (125 traces) not model capacity. The GRU's lead time advantage motivates its probe\n      scores as INPUT to BOCPD, creating a two-stage pipeline: GRU generates probe scores with\n      the best available temporal dynamics, BOCPD detects change points in those scores.\n\n  - name: \"Text-based baseline (sentence-BERT, all models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-013\"\n    description: >\n      Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) embeddings + temporal LR W=15 on CoT text.\n      Extended to 32B models. Compare coverage/lead/accuracy to hidden-state probes.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_text_static_coverage: 0.558\n      r1_32b_text_static_acc: 0.649\n      r1_32b_text_temporal_coverage: 0.628\n      r1_32b_text_temporal_acc: 0.667\n      r1_32b_text_temporal_auroc: 0.742\n      qwq_32b_text_static_coverage: 0.535\n      qwq_32b_text_static_acc: 0.638\n      qwq_32b_text_temporal_coverage: 0.605\n      qwq_32b_text_temporal_acc: 0.654\n      hs_vs_text_acc_gap_r1_32b: 0.187\n      hs_vs_text_acc_gap_qwq_32b: 0.172\n      hs_vs_text_coverage_gap_r1_32b: 0.163\n      hs_vs_text_coverage_gap_qwq_32b: 0.139\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier requires hs_vs_text_acc_gap_32b in [0.12, 0.20]. Text-based probes achieve\n      moderate performance (60-65% coverage) because CoT text does contain safety-relevant\n      lexical cues — words like \"refuse,\" \"harmful,\" \"ethical\" appear in safety reasoning blocks.\n      However, hidden-state probes outperform by +15-19pp accuracy because: (1) hidden states\n      encode safety intent BEFORE it manifests in text (pre-textual signal), (2) hidden states\n      capture the model's internal commitment state, not just surface-level word choice, (3)\n      sentence-BERT is a generic embedding not tuned for safety semantics. The gap is larger on\n      32B models because larger models have richer internal representations that diverge more\n      from surface text — the safety commitment decision is made deeper in the representation\n      hierarchy. This cleanly justifies hidden-state monitoring over cheap text-based alternatives.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 9: Hidden-State vs Text-Based Probes Across Models\"\n        columns: [model, modality, temporal_coverage, temporal_acc, temporal_auroc, acc_gap_vs_text]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, Hidden-state, 0.767, 0.848, 0.872, \"+18.0pp\"]\n          - [r1-8b, Text (SBERT), 0.651, 0.678, 0.759, \"—\"]\n          - [r1-32b, Hidden-state, 0.791, 0.854, 0.891, \"+18.7pp\"]\n          - [r1-32b, Text (SBERT), 0.628, 0.667, 0.742, \"—\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, Hidden-state, 0.744, 0.839, 0.876, \"+17.2pp\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, Text (SBERT), 0.605, 0.654, 0.731, \"—\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 12: Side-by-side ROC curves for hidden-state vs text probes on all 3 models. Hidden-state curves consistently above text curves, with largest gap in the high-specificity (low FPR) region.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The consistent +15-19pp accuracy advantage of hidden-state probes over text-based alternatives\n      is the clearest evidence that hidden-state monitoring provides unique value. The gap is largest\n      in the high-specificity regime (FPR < 0.05), which is exactly where a deployment system would\n      operate — text probes degrade more at strict thresholds because they rely on surface cues that\n      are noisier. This directly addresses the critical reviewer concern: hidden-state monitoring is\n      not redundant with text monitoring.\n\n  - name: \"BOCPD change-point detection on probe scores\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-014\"\n    description: >\n      Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (Adams & MacKay 2007) applied to per-step probe\n      confidence score sequences. Also test CUSUM as simpler alternative. Evaluated on all models.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_bocpd_crossing_rate: 0.721\n      r1_8b_bocpd_lead_mean: 12.4\n      r1_8b_bocpd_lead_median: 7\n      r1_8b_bocpd_lead_std: 11.2\n      r1_8b_bocpd_positive_lead_rate: 0.67\n      r1_32b_bocpd_crossing_rate: 0.698\n      r1_32b_bocpd_lead_mean: 14.1\n      r1_32b_bocpd_lead_median: 9\n      r1_32b_bocpd_positive_lead_rate: 0.71\n      qwq_32b_bocpd_crossing_rate: 0.674\n      qwq_32b_bocpd_lead_mean: 10.8\n      qwq_32b_bocpd_lead_median: 5\n      qwq_32b_bocpd_positive_lead_rate: 0.62\n      cusum_lead_mean: 8.3\n      cusum_lead_median: 4\n      cusum_positive_lead_rate: 0.58\n      bocpd_vs_gru_lead_improvement: \"+7-9 steps mean\"\n      bocpd_vs_gru_positive_lead_improvement: \"+26-30pp\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier requires bocpd_lead_mean [8, 20], bocpd_lead_median [3, 12], bocpd_positive_lead_rate\n      [0.55, 0.8]. BOCPD succeeds where GRU fails because it solves a fundamentally different problem:\n      instead of predicting future safety trajectory from hidden states (what GRU attempts), BOCPD\n      detects distributional SHIFTS in the probe score time series. The probe score sequence exhibits\n      a clear regime change around CP — from high-variance oscillation (safety reasoning present) to\n      monotonic low values (safety reasoning absent). BOCPD is designed exactly for this: detecting\n      when the generative process of a time series changes. The Bayesian framework provides natural\n      uncertainty quantification, and the hazard function prior can be calibrated to the expected\n      CP frequency (~1 per trace). BOCPD achieves positive median lead time (5-9 steps) because\n      the distributional shift begins BEFORE the textual CP — the probe scores start declining as\n      the model's hidden states shift toward commitment even while safety-labeled text is still\n      being generated. This is the \"pre-textual\" signal that justifies hidden-state monitoring.\n      CUSUM achieves slightly lower performance because it uses fixed thresholds rather than Bayesian\n      updating, but still outperforms GRU. The ~5pp crossing rate reduction vs temporal LR (0.67-0.72\n      vs 0.74-0.79) is the cost of requiring a detectable distributional shift — traces where the\n      transition is gradual (no sharp change point) are missed by BOCPD but caught by LR's sliding\n      window.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 10: BOCPD vs GRU vs Temporal LR — Lead Time Comparison\"\n        columns: [model, method, crossing_rate, lead_mean, lead_median, lead_std, positive_lead_rate]\n        rows:\n          - [r1-8b, Temporal LR, 0.767, -1.09, 0, 8.2, \"42%\"]\n          - [r1-8b, GRU multi-7L, 0.767, 4.7, 0, 13.0, \"39%\"]\n          - [r1-8b, BOCPD, 0.721, 12.4, 7, 11.2, \"67%\"]\n          - [r1-8b, CUSUM, 0.698, 8.3, 4, 10.5, \"58%\"]\n          - [r1-32b, Temporal LR, 0.791, 0.3, 0, 8.8, \"44%\"]\n          - [r1-32b, GRU multi-7L, 0.744, 5.2, 0, 14.8, \"41%\"]\n          - [r1-32b, BOCPD, 0.698, 14.1, 9, 12.0, \"71%\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, Temporal LR, 0.744, -0.8, 0, 7.9, \"40%\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, GRU multi-7L, 0.698, 3.9, 0, 12.3, \"36%\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, BOCPD, 0.674, 10.8, 5, 10.8, \"62%\"]\n          - [r1-8b, Oracle, 1.000, 31.0, 29, 15.2, \"100%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 13: Lead time violin plots for GRU vs BOCPD vs Oracle across all models. BOCPD distributions are unimodal and shifted positive; GRU distributions are bimodal with spike at 0.\"\n      - \"Figure 14: BOCPD run-length posterior visualization on 3 example traces — shows change-point probability spiking 5-15 steps before textual CP.\"\n      - \"Figure 15: Coverage-lead time Pareto frontier. Temporal LR dominates coverage axis; BOCPD dominates lead time axis; no single method dominates both.\"\n    analysis: >\n      BOCPD is the breakthrough result in the best tier. It achieves the paper's upgraded Claim 3:\n      reliable pre-textual detection IS possible with the right method. The key insight is that\n      change-point detection on probe scores is complementary to probe accuracy — even an imperfect\n      probe produces score sequences with detectable regime changes. BOCPD's median lead time of\n      5-9 steps means typical detection occurs 5-9 reasoning steps before the textual commitment\n      point, providing a meaningful intervention window. The positive lead rate of 62-71% means\n      the majority of detected traces achieve genuine early detection, versus GRU's 36-41%.\n      This constitutes a genuine methods contribution: applying BOCPD to probe score sequences\n      is novel in the safety detection literature and demonstrably effective. The coverage-lead\n      time tradeoff (BOCPD sacrifices ~5pp coverage for +8-10 step lead time vs temporal LR)\n      suggests a two-stage deployment: temporal LR for high-coverage monitoring, BOCPD for\n      early-warning alerting.\n\n  - name: \"False positive analysis on safe traces\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-015\"\n    description: >\n      Run all probes (static LR, temporal LR, GRU, BOCPD) on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5).\n      Report FPR at operating thresholds, precision-recall curves, deployment utility analysis.\n    expected_results:\n      static_lr_fpr: 0.082\n      temporal_lr_fpr: 0.054\n      gru_fpr: 0.071\n      bocpd_fpr: 0.038\n      temporal_lr_precision_at_80_recall: 0.91\n      temporal_lr_precision_at_90_recall: 0.84\n      bocpd_precision_at_80_recall: 0.94\n      bocpd_precision_at_90_recall: 0.87\n      num_safe_traces: 48\n      safe_trace_max_consecutive_alerts: 2\n    result_rationale: >\n      Best tier requires fpr_at_operating_threshold in [0.02, 0.10]. Safe traces (final_judgment=0.5)\n      contain normal reasoning without self-jailbreak — the model considers the request, may briefly\n      engage safety reasoning, but ultimately provides a safe response. Temporal LR achieves low FPR\n      (5.4%) because the characteristic safety density decline pattern — the monotonic decrease in\n      safety-labeled steps within the window — does not occur in safe traces. Safe traces may\n      show intermittent safety reasoning, but it doesn't PERMANENTLY cease. BOCPD has the lowest\n      FPR (3.8%) because its change-point criterion requires a sustained distributional shift, not\n      just a momentary probe score dip — brief fluctuations in safe traces don't trigger the\n      run-length posterior. Static LR has higher FPR (8.2%) because individual steps in safe traces\n      can incidentally resemble post-CP jailbreak steps (e.g., when the model reasons about the\n      task itself without explicit safety language). GRU's FPR (7.1%) is moderate — it sometimes\n      fires on safe traces where the probe score sequence happens to resemble a commitment trajectory\n      by chance. The safe_trace_max_consecutive_alerts of 2 means that even when false positives\n      occur, they don't persist — a simple debounce (require 3+ consecutive alerts) would reduce\n      effective FPR near zero.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 11: False Positive Analysis on Safe Traces\"\n        columns: [probe, fpr_at_operating_threshold, precision_at_80_recall, precision_at_90_recall, max_consecutive_fp]\n        rows:\n          - [Static LR, 0.082, 0.87, 0.78, 3]\n          - [Temporal LR W=15, 0.054, 0.91, 0.84, 2]\n          - [GRU multi-7L, 0.071, 0.89, 0.81, 2]\n          - [BOCPD, 0.038, 0.94, 0.87, 1]\n          - [CUSUM, 0.045, 0.92, 0.85, 2]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 16: Precision-recall curves for all probes. BOCPD and temporal LR dominate — high precision maintained even at 90% recall.\"\n      - \"Figure 17: Probe score time series on 4 example safe traces vs 4 jailbreak traces. Safe traces show stable oscillation; jailbreak traces show clear transition to sustained low scores.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Low FPR across all probes confirms that coverage metrics are deployment-meaningful — high\n      coverage is not achieved by simply alerting on everything. BOCPD's lowest FPR (3.8%) combined\n      with its lead time advantage makes it the recommended deployment method for early warning.\n      Temporal LR's slightly higher FPR (5.4%) but better coverage (0.77 vs 0.72) makes it the\n      recommended method for comprehensive monitoring. The two methods serve complementary deployment\n      roles. The precision-at-90%-recall of 0.84-0.87 means that in deployment, fewer than 1 in 6\n      alerts would be false positives, which is operationally acceptable for a safety monitoring system.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"47a9f493-e1d7-4c88-83ab-6d41811d63f4","total_cost_usd":0.71227765,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15732,"cache_read_input_tokens":59935,"output_tokens":16440,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15732,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":16153,"cache_read_input_tokens":24173,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3400,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3400},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":7113,"outputTokens":5385,"cacheReadInputTokens":535309,"cacheCreationInputTokens":68317,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.17296515000000004,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":16440,"cacheReadInputTokens":59935,"cacheCreationInputTokens":15732,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.5393125,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"6e337f52-7df8-4297-8f1f-c2f7fcdf86b5"}
